---
# Closes the exposure rbac.yaml.j2's header describes: Authelia guards the IngressRoute, but the
# ClusterIP Service does not go through Traefik at all. Headlamp runs with
# `-unsafe-use-service-account-token` (deployment.yaml.j2), so anything that reaches the pod acts
# as the headlamp SA — a cluster-wide `view` binding. Any pod in {{ k8s_namespace }} that
# processes untrusted input (qbittorrent, karakeep's headless chrome, the n8n runners) could read
# every workload, node, PV, CRD and RBAC binding without passing Traefik, Authelia or CrowdSec.
#
# INGRESS, the direction proven enforced on this cluster — an Egress policy selects pods
# correctly and blocks nothing (measured 2026-08-07). netpol-probe-job.yaml.j2 beside this file
# re-checks the policy on every deploy rather than trusting that it took.
#
# This does not narrow the RBAC, which is the other half of the exposure and is deliberately
# read-only: the SA holds no secrets, no pods/exec and no write verbs (rbac.yaml.j2, enforced by
# ansible/tests/test_k8s_manifests.py).
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: headlamp
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: headlamp
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    # Traefik is the only legitimate caller: every human path to Headlamp arrives through the
    # IngressRoute, where the Authelia middleware runs. Kubelet's probe traffic needs no rule —
    # it reaches the pod IP directly and kube-router does not subject that path to ingress
    # enforcement (flaresolverr has run on probes under an equally strict policy since
    # 2026-08-13).
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: traefik
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ container_item.port }}
